<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8" ?>

<section xml:id="section-triangles" >
<title>Triangles</title>

    <subsection>
        <title>Basic Triangle Theorems</title>

        <p>Note all theorems in this section can and should be proved without using the parallel postulate.</p>

        <definition xml:id="def-vertical-angles">
            <title>Vertical Angles</title>
            <statement><p>The opposing angles formed by the intersection of two lines are called <term>vertical angles</term>.</p></statement>
        </definition>

        <definition>
            <title>Congruent Angles</title>
            <statement><p>Two angles are <term>congruent</term> (<m>\angle ABC \cong \angle DEF</m>) if and only if their measures are equal (<m>m\angle ABC = m\angle DEF</m>).</p></statement>
        </definition>

        <theorem>
            <title>Vertical Angle Congruence</title>
            <statement><p>Vertical angles are congruent.</p></statement>
            <proof>
                <image source="images/asset-2-1-3-submission.png">
                    <shortdescription>Image required for the proof of Theorem 2-1-3.</shortdescription>
                </image>

                <p>Take the figure above. Because angles <m>\alpha</m> and <m>\gamma</m> form a linear pair, the supplement postulate indicates that <m>m(\angle\alpha)+m(\angle\gamma)=180</m> <degree/>. We also know that <m>\gamma</m> and <m>\beta</m> form a linear pair, so we know that <m>m(\angle\gamma)+m(\angle\beta)=180</m> <degree/>.</p>

                <p>
                Thus,
                <m>m(\angle\alpha)+m(\angle\gamma)=m(\angle\gamma)+m(\angle\beta)</m>
                So, <m>m(\angle\alpha)=m(\angle\beta)</m>.
                Thus, <m>\angle\alpha\cong\angle\beta</m>.
                We can show by similar processes that
                <m>\angle\gamma\cong\angle\sigma</m>, so the theorem is proven.
                </p>
            </proof>
        </theorem>

        <p><m>A-B-C</m> means that the points <m>A,B,</m> and <m>C</m> are colinear and <m>B</m> is between <m>A</m> and <m>C.</m></p>

        <theorem>
            <title>Pasch's Axiom</title>
            <statement><p>If a line <m>\ell</m> intersects a triangle <m>\triangle ABC</m> at a point <m>D</m> such that <m>A-D-B,</m> then <m>\ell</m> must intersect <m>\overline{AC}</m> or <m>\overline{BC}.</m></p></statement>
            <proof>
                <p>
                    Assume there is some arbitrary triangle <m>\triangle ABC</m> with a point D such that A-B-D, with a line <m>\ell</m> that passes through point D. We apply the plane separation postulate using <m>\ell</m> as our line and the fact that a plane contains at least three non-collinear points. By the plane separation postulate, we can create two sets {A,B} and {C}. By the plane separation postulate, this means that segment <m>\overline{AC}</m> and <m>\overline{BC}</m> intersect the line <m>\ell</m>. Therefore, <m>\ell</m> must intersect <m>\overline{AC}</m> or <m>\overline{BC}</m>. 
                </p>
            </proof>
        </theorem>

        <theorem>
            <title>Crossbar</title>
            <statement><p>If <m>X</m> is a point in the interior of <m>\triangle ABC</m> then ray <m>AX</m> intersects <m>\overline{BC}</m> at a point <m>D</m> such that <m>B-D-C.</m></p></statement>
        </theorem>

        <definition>
            <title>Congruent Line Segments</title>
            <statement><p>Two line segments are congruent (<m>\overline{AB} \cong \overline{CD}</m>) if and only if their measures (length) are equal (<m>|AB|=|CD|</m>).</p></statement>
        </definition>

        <definition>
            <title>Isosceles</title>
            <statement><p>A triangle is <term>isosceles</term> if and only if two sides are congruent.</p></statement>
        </definition>

        <theorem>
            <title>Isosceles Triangle</title>
            <statement><p>In an isosceles triangle the angles opposite the equal sides are congruent.</p></statement>
            <proof>
                <p>
                    Consider <m>\triangle ABC</m> with <m>AB \cong AC</m>.
                    <image src="images/triangle.png" />
                </p>
                <p>
                    Draw an angle bisector from <m>A</m> to the
                    base <m>BC</m>, meeting <m>BC</m> at point <m>D</m>.
                    <image src="images/triangles.png" />
                </p>
                <p>
                    <m>\angle BAD \cong \angle CAD</m> from the construction of <m>AD</m>.
                </p>

                <p>
                    <m>AD \cong AD</m> by the reflexive property. 
                </p>

                <p>
                    <m>\triangle ABD \cong \triangle ACD</m> by the Side-Angle-Side congruence postulate.
                </p>
                <p>
                    Corresponding angles of congruent triangles are congruent, so
                    <m>\angle B \cong \angle C</m>. Therefore, the angles opposite the congruent
                    sides of an isosceles triangle are congruent.
                </p>
            </proof>
        </theorem>

        <theorem>
            <title>Perpendicular Bisector</title>
            <statement><p>A point is on the perpendicular bisector of a segment if and only if it is equidistant from the endpoints.</p></statement>
            <proof>
                <p>
                ( <m>\Rightarrow</m> ) Let <m>\overline{BC}</m> be a line segment.
                Construct a perpendicular bisector that intersects <m>\overline{BC}</m> at
                a point <m>D</m> so that <m>B-D-C</m>. Place a point <m>A</m> on this
                perpendicular bisector. By Axiom 1, we can construct unique line segments
                <m>\overline{AB}</m> and <m>\overline{AC}</m>. Since
                <m>\overleftrightarrow{AD}</m> is a perpendicular bisector to
                <m>\overline{BC}</m>, we have <m>\overline{BD}\cong\overline{DC}</m>. By
                reflexivity, <m>\overline{AD}\cong\overline{AD}</m>. In fact, since
                <m>\overleftrightarrow{AD}</m> is a perpendicular bisector of
                <m>\overline{BC}</m>, we know that <m>m\angle ADB=m\angle ADC=\pi/2</m>.
                Hence, <m>\angle ADB\cong\angle ADC</m>. By the SAS Postulate,
                <m>\triangle ADB</m> is in a one-to-one correspondence to
                <m>\triangle ACD</m>. So, <m>\overline{AB}\cong\overline{AC}</m> and
                therefore <m>|AB|=|AC|</m>, as desired.
                </p>
            </proof>
        </theorem>

        <definition>
            <title>Exterior Angle</title>
            <statement><p>The supplementary angle formed by extending one side of a triangle is called an <term>exterior angle</term>.</p></statement>
        </definition>

        <theorem>
            <title>Exterior Angle</title>
            <statement><p>The measure of an exterior angle of a triangle is greater than the measure of either of the opposing angles of the triangle.</p></statement>
            <proof>
                <p>Suppose we have <m>\triangle ABC</m>. We can construct a ray from         <m>\angle ACB</m> such that it goes through <m>\overline{AB}</m> and intersects it at point <m>E</m>, such that <m>A-E-B</m> and <m>E</m> is the midpoint of <m>\overline{AB}</m>. Thus, <m>\overline{AE} \cong \overline{EB}</m>.</p>

                <p>By the Ruler Postulate, we then construct point <m>F</m> on the ray such that <m>C-E-F</m>, with <m>E</m> as the midpoint of <m>\overline{CF}</m>. Therefore, <m>\overline{EF} \cong \overline{CE}</m>.</p>

                <image source="images/exterior_angle3.png"/>

                <p>By the Vertical Angle Theorem, <m>\angle FEB \cong \angle AEC</m>. Since
                <m>\overline{CE} \cong \overline{EF}</m> and <m>\overline{AE} \cong \overline{EB}</m>, it follows by SAS that <md>\triangle AEC \cong \triangle FEB.</md> Thus, <md>\angle CAE \cong \angle EBF.</md> By the Angle Addition Postulate, 
                    <md>
                    <mrow>
                        m\angle ABD
                        &amp;= m\angle EBF + m\angle FBD
                    </mrow>
                    <mrow>
                        &amp;= m\angle CAE + m\angle FBD
                    </mrow>
                    <mrow>
                        &amp;= m\angle CAB + m\angle FBD.
                    </mrow>
                    </md>
                By Axiom 12, <m>m\angle FBD \ne 0</m>.
                </p>
            </proof>
        </theorem>

        <definition>
            <title>Congruent Triangles</title>
            <statement><p>Two triangles are congruent if and only if all their sides and angles are congruent (<m>\Delta ABC \cong \Delta DEF</m>).</p></statement>
        </definition>
    </subsection>

    <subsection>
        <title>Triangle Congruence Theorems</title>

        <exercise><p>Determine if two triangles with two congruent sides and a congruent angle not between the two sides are congruent.</p></exercise>

            <theorem>
                <title>Angle-Side-Angle</title>
                <statement><p>Two triangles are congruent if and only if two corresponding angles and the side between them are congruent.</p></statement>
            </theorem>

            <theorem>
                <title>Angle-Angle-Side</title>
                <statement><p>Two triangles are congruent if and only if two corresponding angles and a side not between them are congruent.</p></statement>
                <proof>

                    <p>Suppose we have two triangles, <m>\triangle ABC</m> and <m>\triangle DEF</m>, with <m>\angle BAC \cong \angle EDF</m>, <m>\angle ACB \cong \angle DFE</m>, and <m>\overline{AB} \cong \overline{DE}</m>.

                    <image source="images/aas.png"/>

                    Assume, for contradiction, that <m>m\angle ABC \gt m\angle DEF</m>. We want to construct an angle <m>\alpha</m> such that <m>\angle \alpha \cong \angle E</m>. Using Axiom 18, we can construct a crossbar from <m>\angle B</m> that intersects <m>\overline{AC}</m>, constructing a point <m>G</m> such that <m>A-G-C</m>.</p>
                    <p>We now also have <m>\angle \alpha \cong \angle E</m>.

                    <image source="images/aas2.png"/>

                    By doing this, through angle-side-angle, the <m>\triangle ABG</m> that we constructed is congruent to <m>\triangle DEF</m> and we have <m>\angle AGB \cong \angle GCB \cong \angle DFE</m>. But <m>\angle AGB</m> is an exterior angle of <m>\angle BGC</m>, by the exterior angle theorem, <m>m\angle AGB \gt m\angle GCB</m> and <m>m\angle DFE</m> which contradicts the definition of congruent triangles.</p>
                </proof>
            </theorem>

            <theorem>
                <title>Side-Side-Side</title>
                <statement><p>Two triangles are congruent if and only if all three corresponding sides are congruent.</p></statement>
                <proof>
                    <p>
                    ( <m>\Rightarrow</m> ) Construct triangles <m>\triangle ABC</m> and
                    <m>\triangle DEF</m> such that
                    <md alignment="align">
                        <mrow>\overline{AB}&amp;\cong\overline{DE},</mrow>
                        <mrow>\overline{BC}&amp;\cong\overline{EF},</mrow>
                        <mrow>\overline{AC}&amp;\cong\overline{DF}.</mrow>
                    </md>
                    By the Angle Construction Postulate, we can construct a ray
                    <m>\overrightarrow{AP}</m> such that <m>m\angle PAB=m\angle FDE</m>, where
                    <m>P</m> and <m>C</m> are in separate half-planes separated by
                    <m>\overleftrightarrow{AB}</m>. Then, on ray <m>\overrightarrow{AP}</m>,
                    by the Ruler Postulate we can extend this ray and place a point <m>F'</m>
                    such that <m>\overline{AF'}\cong\overline{DF}</m>. By Axiom 1, we can
                    construct the line <m>\overline{F'B}</m>. Now, since
                    <m>\overline{AF'}\cong\overline{DF}</m>,
                    <m>\overline{AB}\cong\overline{DE}</m>, and
                    <m>\angle F'AB\cong\angle FDE</m>, we know that
                    <m>\triangle ABF'\cong\triangle DEF</m> by the SAS Postulate. For the sake
                    of simplicity, we will re-label <m>\triangle ABF'</m> as
                    <m>\triangle DEF</m>. So, we should have the following figure.
                    </p>

                    <image width="75%" source="images/asset-2-1.png"/>

                    <p>
                    Essentially, we place <m>\triangle DEF</m> on <m>\triangle ABC</m> such
                    that <m>D</m> overlaps <m>A</m> and <m>E</m> overlaps <m>B</m>. By axiom
                    1, we can construct the segment <m>\overline{CF}</m>.
                    </p>

                    <image width="75%" source="images/asset-2-1-2.png"/>

                    <p>
                    We observe the new triangles <m>\triangle CDF</m> and
                    <m>\triangle CBF</m>. Since <m>\overline{AC}\cong\overline{DF}</m> and
                    <m>\overline{BC}\cong\overline{EF}</m>, triangles <m>\triangle CAF</m> and
                    <m>\triangle CBF</m> are isosceles. By the Isosceles Triangle Theorem,
                    <m>\angle ACF\cong\angle DFC</m> and <m>\angle BCF\cong\angle EFC</m>.
                    </p>

                    <image width="75%" source="images/asset-2-1-3.png"/>

                    <p>
                    Since <m>F</m> lies in the interior of <m>\angle ACB</m> and <m>C</m> lies
                    in the interior of <m>\angle DFE</m>, by the Angle Addition Postulate,
                    <md alignment="align">
                        <mrow>m\angle ACB&amp;=m\angle ACF+m\angle BCF,</mrow>
                        <mrow>m\angle DFE&amp;=m\angle DFC+m\angle EFC.</mrow>
                    </md>
                    Using the fact that <m>\angle ACF\cong\angle DFC</m> and
                    <m>\angle BCF\cong\angle EFC</m>, we see that
                    <md alignment="align">
                        <mrow>m\angle ACB&amp;=m\angle DFC+m\angle EFC=m\angle DFE.</mrow>
                    </md>
                    So, we have that
                    <ol>
                        <li>
                        <p>
                            <m>\overline{AC}\cong\overline{DF}</m>,
                        </p>
                        </li>

                        <li>
                        <p>
                            <m>\overline{BC}\cong\overline{EF}</m>,
                        </p>
                        </li>

                        <li>
                        <p>
                            <m>\angle ACB\cong\angle DFE</m>.
                        </p>
                        </li>
                    </ol>
                    By the SAS Postulate, <m>\triangle ABC\cong\triangle DEF</m>.
                    </p>

                    <p>
                    ( <m>\Leftarrow</m> ) Suppose <m>\triangle ABC\cong\triangle DEF</m>.
                    Then, by definition, their corresponding sides are congruent. So,
                    <md alignment="align">
                        <mrow>\overline{AB}\cong\overline{DE},</mrow>
                        <mrow>\overline{BC}\cong\overline{EF},</mrow>
                        <mrow>\overline{AC}\cong\overline{DF}.</mrow>
                    </md>
                    </p>
                </proof>
            </theorem>

            <theorem>
                <title>Right Angle-Side-Side</title>
                <statement><p>Two right triangles are congruent if and only if two corresponding sides and a right angle not between those sides are congruent.</p></statement>
                <proof>
                    <image source="images/asset-2-1-17-image.png">
                        <shortdescription>Image required for the proof of right angle side side theorem</shortdescription>
                    </image>

                    <p>Take the above figure and suppose that line segments <m>i</m> and <m>j</m> to be congruent and the angles <m>\angle CBA</m> and <m>\angle DBA</m> to be congruent. Note that line segment <m>f</m> is congruent to itself. Then, per the Isosceles Triangle Theorem, <m>\angle ACB\cong\angle ADB</m>. Thus, by our congruences, we know that <m>\triangle ABC\cong\triangle ABD</m> per Angle Angle Side congruence.</p>
                </proof>
            </theorem>

            <theorem>
                <title>Converse of Isosceles Triangle</title>
                <statement><p>If two angles of a triangle are congruent then the sides opposite those angles are congruent.</p></statement>
            </theorem>

            <theorem>
                <title>Extended Inverse of Isosceles Triangle</title>
                <statement><p>If two sides of a triangle are not congruent then the angles opposite those sides are not congruent. Further the larger angle is opposite the longer side.</p></statement>
            </theorem>

            <theorem>
                <title>Triangle Inequality</title>
                <statement><p>The sum of the lengths of any two sides of a triangle is larger than the length of the other side.</p></statement>
                <proof>
                    <p>
                        Case 1: Where 1 side is longer than the others. 
                        <image width="75%" source="images/triangle-inequality.jpg"/>
                        <p>
                            There exists some arbitrary triangle <m>\triangle ABC</m>. Assume that <m>\overline{AB}</m> is the longest side. 
                        </p>
                        
                        <p>
                            Subcase 1: <m>|AB|+|AC| \gt |BC|</m>
                            <p>
                            The length of <m>\overline{AC}</m> is a non-negative number and is being added to the longest side <m>\overline{AB}</m>. The sum will remain longer than the other side. 
                            <p><m>|AB| \gt |BC|</m> then <m>|AB|+|AC| \gt |BC|</m> remains true.  
                            </p>
                            </p>
                        </p>
                        <p>
                            Subcase 2: <m>|AB|+|BC| \gt |AC|</m>
                            <p>
                            Similar to Subcase 1. 
                            </p>
                        </p>
                        <p>
                            Subcase 3: <m>|AC|+|BC| \gt |AB|</m>
                            <p>
                            Place a point D such that <m>|AD|=|AC|</m>. We need to show that <m>|BC| \gt |DB|</m> for the sum of the two sides to be greater than <m>|AB|</m>. By definition, <m>\triangle ADC</m> is an isosceles triangle and by the Isosceles Triangle theorem, the angles opposite the equal sides are congruent. <m>m\angle ADC=m\angle DCA</m>. 
                            </p>
                            <image width="75%" source="images/triangle-inequality2.jpg"/>
                            <p> 
                            <m>\angle ADC</m> is an exterior angle to <m>\triangle BDC</m>  and by the exterior angle theorem, <m>m\angle ADC \gt m\angle BCD</m>. <m>\angle BDC</m> is an exterior angle to <m>\triangle ADC</m>, so <m>m\angle BDC \gt m\angle DCA</m>. We know that <m>\angle ADC\cong\angle DCA</m> which  means <m>m\angle BDC \gt m\angle ADC</m> but we also know that <m>m\angle ADC \gt m\angle BCD</m>. Therefore, <m>m\angle BDC \gt m\angle BCD</m>. By the extended inverse of isosceles triangle, <m>|BC| \gt |DB|</m>. 
                            </p>
                            <p>
                            <m>|AB|=|AD|+|DB|</m> this equation becomes <m>|AB|=|AC|+|DB|</m>. Since we know that <m>|BC| \gt |DB|</m> this means <m>|AC|+|BC| \gt |AB|</m> which is what we wanted to prove.  
                            </p>
                        </p>
                    </p>
                    <p>
                        Case 2: 2 or more sides are equal
                    </p>
                    <p> 
                        This means for some <m>\triangle ABC</m> where <m>|AB|=|AC|=|BC|</m> then for the sum of any two sides, <m>2|AB| \gt |AB|</m> which proves the theorem. 
                        </p>
                </proof>
            </theorem>

    </subsection>

</section>
